sec.p:
To determine the free fraction of paliperidone and risperidone in plasma samples, Centrifree Ultrafiltration Devices from Merck Millipore (Amsterdam, The Netherlands) were used to separate the free from the protein bound risperidone and paliperidone in pooled plasma samples. Both the ultrafiltrate and the original pooled plasma sample (without ultrafiltration step) were measured. The free fraction was calculated according to the following Eq. 1:1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ Free\  fraction=\frac{Ultrafiltrate\  concentration}{Pooled\  plasma\  concentration} $$\end{document}Freefraction=UltrafiltrateconcentrationPooledplasmaconcentration

The in-vivo recovery of paliperidone, risperidone phenytoin and remoxipride was calculated using the compound concentration in the dialysate (Cdial) and in PF (Cin) according to the following Eq. 2:2\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ In\  vivo\  recovery=\frac{C_{in}-{C}_{dial}}{C_{in}} $$\end{document}Invivorecovery=Cin−CdialCin

For the 6 compounds, data were obtained using co-administration of inhibitors of active transport. For all these compounds, the effect of the active transport inhibitors was tested on drug exchange at the BBB (QPL_ECF) and plasma clearance (CLPL), and in combination, as a categorical covariate. (Eq.3)3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ P={P}_{PAT}\times \left(1+{\theta}_{cov}\cdot Cov\right) $$\end{document}P=PPAT×1+θcov⋅Covwhere PPAT represents the parameter including passive and active transport (net transport), P represents the parameter which takes into account the active transport inhibitors if there is any such effect, Cov is the value of the covariate (0: without an active transport inhibitor, 1: with an active transport inhibitor), θcov represents the effect of the active transport inhibitor.

The systematic inclusion of aforementioned factors was guided by a likelihood ratio test, by an adequate parameter estimation precision, by assessment of the parameter correlation matrix to ensure parameter identifiability, and by the graphical evaluation of plots for observations versus predictions and weighted residuals versus time and versus predictions. The likelihood ratio test is based on the assumption that changes in the NONMEM objective function values (OFV, -2 log likelihood) are asymptotically chi-square distributed. A decrease of OFV ≥ 3.84 was considered statistically significant (p < 0.05). For a clear assessment of model predictions and observations we also computed the following metrics (Eq.4 and 5).4\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ PE=\frac{Y_{OBS,ij}-{Y}_{PRED,ij}}{Y_{OBS,ij}-{Y}_{PRED,ij}/2} $$\end{document}PE=YOBS,ij−YPRED,ijYOBS,ij−YPRED,ij/2
5\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ SMAPE=\frac{1}{N}{\displaystyle {\sum}_{k=1}^N\left|PE\right|\times 100} $$\end{document}SMAPE=1N∑k=1NPE×100where PE is a prediction error, and SMAPE is symmetric mean absolute percentage error (43). YOBS,ij is the jth observation of the ith subject, YPRED,ij is the jth prediction of the ith subject. N is number of observations. In the cases where we did not estimate inter-individual variability, e.g. for all brain PK data, YPRED,ij equals the mean population prediction YPRED,j.

The plasma-related parameters including inter-individual variability on these parameters and residual errors were estimated using the external rat plasma data. We used a mixed effects modeling approach to investigate the predictability of the brain concentration based on each plasma concentration. The same plasma model structure, which was obtained from the brain PK model was applied for each compound. Inter-individual variability were tested on each PK parameter using an exponential model (Eq. 6).6\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ {\theta}_i=\theta \times {e}^{\upeta_{\mathrm{i}}} $$\end{document}θi=θ×eηiwhere θi represents the parameters of the ith subject, θ represents the population mean value of the parameter, and ηi is the random effect of the ith subject under the assumption of a normal distribution with a mean value of 0 and variance of ω2.

A proportional error model and the mixed error model (Eq. 7-8) were tested for the residual errors:7\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
